# Project 19: the four partition card of projects/19-rauc-ab/docs/DESIGN.md
# figure 2. This is the first .wks in this repository; every other image
# here uses the BSP's stock one partition layout, which cannot express a
# second root slot.
#
#   p1  boot   64 MiB   vfat   firmware, u-boot.bin, boot.scr, uboot.env, dtbs
#   p2  rootA  1024 MiB ext4   slot A, read-only at runtime
#   p3  rootB  1024 MiB ext4   slot B, empty until the first rauc install
#   p4  data   512 MiB  ext4   /etc upper, /var, rauc.status, bundles
#
# BOTH SLOTS ARE --fixed-size AND EQUAL, and that is not a stylistic
# choice. RAUC writes a whole filesystem image into a partition rather than
# syncing files into it. A slot that is allowed to grow to fill the card is
# a slot that stops matching its twin: if the target is smaller than the
# image the install fails outright, and if it is larger the difference is
# wasted on every update for the life of the device. --size would give the
# first behaviour on one card and the second on another, decided by
# whatever card happened to be in the reader.
#
# THE KERNEL IS DELIBERATELY NOT ON p1. On a Raspberry Pi the kernel
# normally lives on the FAT partition and the firmware loads it directly,
# which would make it shared between the slots and therefore not updatable.
# Figure 1 has U-Boot ext4load the kernel out of the slot it has chosen, so
# each slot carries its own /boot/Image and an update can change the
# kernel. bench-ab-image.bb installs kernel-image-image for exactly this
# reason and says so there too.
#
# What p1 holds is shared and is NOT updatable by any bundle: the GPU
# firmware, u-boot.bin, boot.scr, the device tree and uboot.env. That is
# the design's main limitation rather than an oversight, and it is why the
# project keeps a second microSD card.
#
# 64 + 1024 + 1024 + 512 is 2624 MiB, so the smallest card this fits is a
# 4 GB one. The spare space at the end is left unpartitioned on purpose:
# growing a slot later is a partition table edit, and leaving room makes
# that edit possible without moving p4 and its persistent state.

part /boot --source bootimg-partition --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype=vfat --label boot --active --align 4096 --size 64

part / --source rootfs --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype=ext4 --label rootA --align 4096 --fixed-size 1024M

# No --source. Slot B is an empty ext4 filesystem on a freshly flashed
# card, and stays empty until the first bundle is installed into it.
# Booting it before then fails, the boot script spends an attempt, and the
# board returns to slot A, which is the mechanism working rather than a
# fault.
part --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype=ext4 --label rootB --align 4096 --fixed-size 1024M

# Shared, persistent, and owned by neither slot. Everything that must
# survive an update lives here, which is also what makes it the one
# partition a rollback cannot undo. docs/DESIGN.md records that as a
# hazard: the schema of /data must not change between bundles, because
# slot A and slot B both read it and only one of them was built against
# the new shape.
part /data --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype=ext4 --label data --align 4096 --fixed-size 512M

bootloader --ptable msdos
